  * (userland/bt/btbridge.c), which connects to the privileged control "com.sweet.bt.host". Bytes sent by the iOS
  * side go to the bridge and the bridge's go to the iOS side (the most recent iOS connection; BlueTool and
  * bluetoothd take turns). The termios options are kept and answered (the link speed is the bridge's business).
+ * 0222: bluetoothd's stack does not read or write that socket: it asks it for a Skywalk channel and moves the bytes
+ * through the channel's rings (research/bluetooth/transport.md, H4 open callback): getsockopt 0x40487413 (termios),
+ * setsockopt 0x800c5414 (12 bytes: open the channel), getsockopt 0x40105412 (16 bytes: the channel's nexus UUID),
+ * os_channel_create, then setsockopt 0x80487414 (termios). The channel is a kernel-pipe nexus as XNU's utun makes
+ * one (bsd/net/if_utun.c utun_enable_channel / utun_kpipe_sync_tx / _rx), bound to the asking process; its slots
+ * carry the H4 byte stream in chunks (no packet boundaries), so the bridge sees the same bytes either way.
